Owner-guaranteed resource lifetimes and change-driven execution — implementation plan
===================================================================================

Source proposal: [Rendering.BindingAdmission.Proposal.md](Rendering.BindingAdmission.Proposal.md).
Status: phases 1–2 complete. Phases 3–7 pending; operation-family execution migration remains pending.
Related work: Rendering.GpuCommands.todo. This plan does not complete its outstanding qualification tasks.

Goal
  Establish correctness at creation, preparation and owner-controlled lifecycle changes across all resource
  families. Keep consumer references non-owning; remove repeated execution-time lifetime proof and stable
  compatibility validation. Preserve metadata, actual operation effects, external observation and GPU-safe
  retirement. The source proposal controls semantics; this file controls order and completion evidence.

Mandatory implementation boundaries
  - GpuResourceRef is conceptual terminology, not a request to add a new base type. GpuTextureRef,
    GpuBufferRef and other consumer selections remain non-owning and expose no lifecycle authority.
  - No admitted dependency sets, execution groups, activation locks, universal admission state machine,
    duplicate metadata registry or general operation validator. Existing owner synchronization and genuine
    mapping/completion protocols are not forbidden by this restriction.
  - Owners guarantee lifetimes and legal mutation intervals. Reference capture, command recording and
    preparation do not acquire ownership or block disposal. Development validation diagnoses misuse;
    ordinary Release is not required to detect all owner/consumer contract violations.
  - Retain operational failure handling, mandatory managed memory safety, native outcome handling and
    completion-safe retirement. Do not blanket-remove guards without identifying their actual purpose.
  - Migrate each operation family with its callers, blocking dependencies, external integration, tests and
    obsolete API removal. No intermediate forwarding facades, aliases, compatibility shims or parallel
    execution authorities. If dependency closure crosses a later phase, bring that work into the current
    unit or merge units before implementing; never complete a unit against a temporary path.
  - Unmigrated families can retain their original implementation until their unit is migrated. A completed
    unit must use final boundaries and must not depend on later work for correct owner lifetimes or refresh.
  - Preserve OpenGL 4.5, backend-neutral RHI descriptions, explicit caller synchronization and exactly-once
    non-throwing external native forwarding. No automatic barriers, new hazard scheduler or native retry.
  - Compact binding loops/state comparisons are acceptable. No requirement for constant-time drawing,
    zero traversal, persistent plans for unique transfers or a dependency node for every use.
  - Planning and implementation do not authorize staging or commits. All build/test tools run through
    subagents. Do not launch the game; user-run runtime evidence remains distinct from automated evidence.
  - No mandatory timing baseline or benchmark. Use focused behavior and operation-count checks. Batch
    coherent changes before validation; repeat checks only for relevant changes or unresolved failures.

Implementation decisions to resolve within scope
  - Phase 1 identifies the actual owner and legal release/mutation boundary for every consumer, including
    recorded work and engine-owned resources. Current compliance must be established, not assumed.
  - Exact preparation types, dirty-state representation and diagnostic hooks are implementation choices.
    Reuse existing mechanisms; do not treat this plan as authorization for a new generalized framework.
  - Follow the repository's DEBUG-only diagnostic convention. Operational behavior remains supported in
    ordinary Release. Any necessary contract change beyond the approved proposal requires discussion.

Phase 1: Map ownership, checks and complete migration units
  Outcome: every resource/operation family has an accountable owner and an executable migration route.
  [x] Inventory creation/import, storage/configuration, shader/pipeline, resource binding, geometry/indirect,
      framebuffer/pass, upload/copy/clear/mips, mapping/query/sync/readback and retirement entry points.
      Include immediate, recorded, cached, engine-owned, local/tool and internal implementation resources.
  [x] Identify each owner, non-owning consumer, required use interval, release path and mutation schedule.
      Cover worker disposal requests, reentrant callbacks, shader reload, resize, world exit and context loss.
      Distinguish managed wrapper reachability, native resource lifetime and GPU storage completion.
  [x] Classify every repeated check/guard as creation/preparation establishment, relevant-change refresh,
      dynamic handling, operational failure, managed memory safety or development diagnostic.
      Name checks to remove and protocols to keep; include helpers reached through reference accessors.
  [x] Trace BeginBindingExecution, ResourceBindingValidation, ShaderPreparedSubmission, ShaderInputValidation,
      OpenGLBindingPlan, BindingReuse and OpenGlStateCache.BindingPublication to their actual owners/callers.
      Extend the inventory beyond binding code; no family may be omitted because it uses a different helper.
  [x] Establish the dependency closure for phases 2–6 and map every proposal section to an implementation
      task and focused verification. Identify tests currently demanding universal Release misuse rejection.
      Record findings in temporary docs/Rendering.BindingAdmission.Migrations.md; do not create a second
      authoritative design. Record concrete APIs/files and any phase merging required by dependency closure.
  [x] Review the map against the full proposal and current code. Separate established facts from unknown
      owner behavior. Resolve missing legal lifetime boundaries before removing their defensive checks.
  [x] Completion gate: every family and check has an owner, disposition and migration unit; no unit relies
      on a later fix or shim, and no unapproved ownership/admission mechanism has been introduced.

  Completion evidence: source inventory in [Rendering.BindingAdmission.Migrations.md](Rendering.BindingAdmission.Migrations.md).
  Second review and independent completion audit passed. Documentation-only work; no runtime edits or
  build/test run required. Owner corrections remain assigned to phases 2–6; later phases stay pending.

Phase 2: Establish owner lifecycle and diagnostic boundaries
  Outcome: resource consumers can rely on explicit owner sequencing before their execution paths change.
  [x] Correct identified owner lifecycle gaps at the owning creation, replacement, release and teardown sites.
      Preserve non-owning reference semantics and original associations; do not add consumer AddRef/Release
      behavior, hidden lifetime extension or a new resource hierarchy.
  [x] Make cached consumers withdraw or rebuild before incompatible replacement/release. Make recorded-work
      owners preserve dependencies until execution or discard, without having recording acquire ownership.
      Cover immediate execution, pass attachments and actual mapping/asynchronous lifetime intervals.
  [x] Route worker disposal through existing owner scheduling and prevent legal callbacks from destroying
      resources in their enclosing use interval. Do not solve this with per-draw guard or group activation.
  [x] Establish engine integration use intervals from engine lifecycle boundaries; refresh or withdraw
      consumers on replacement/retirement. Cover local/tool resources and internally owned samplers/buffers.
      Interception updates knowledge but never supplies ownership or vetoes native calls.
  [x] Preserve context-correct deletion, GPU completion tickets, allocator last-use and deferred reuse.
      CPU command completion must not be substituted for GPU completion.
  [x] Establish optional DEBUG validation at appropriate boundaries for stale references, ownership misuse,
      wrong context/thread and internal consistency. Reuse diagnostic infrastructure where available;
      diagnostics must neither repair state nor be necessary for valid Release execution.
  [x] Update tests for owner withdrawal, recording/discard, replacement, worker scheduling and teardown.
      Move representative misuse expectations to diagnostics; retain operational-failure and memory-safety
      coverage in Release. Do not weaken unrelated numerical or rendering assertions.
  [x] Reconcile Rendering.GpuResources.md and applicable submission contracts with implemented owner behavior;
      leave not-yet-migrated execution changes in this plan, not as completed permanent documentation.
  [x] Completion gate: focused owner/lifecycle checks pass and concrete scheduling is correct for the upcoming
      consumers. No existing execution guard is removed solely on the assumption that owners are compliant.

  Completion evidence: second review and independent completion audit passed. Focused Debug and ordinary
  Release each passed 119/119 tests, zero skipped. Receipts: TestResults/BindingOwner/
  binding-owner-debug-final.trx and binding-owner-release-final.trx. Original-service capture, worker
  retirement, recording/discard, lifecycle reentry, alias identity and teardown are covered. Existing
  operation-family guards remain for phases 3–6; no live-game or performance acceptance is claimed.

Phase 3: Establish producer invariants and targeted preparation refresh
  Outcome: creation and changes publish reliable facts for family-specific prepared consumers.
  [ ] Remove duplicate supplied-description checks across factories, manager publication and backend creation
      where no distinct contract requires them. Validate initialization/import once at the right boundary;
      preserve necessary native outcome checks and cleanup, with no provisional usable resource on failure.
  [ ] Apply the same principles to storage/configuration and typed views, shaders, executables and pipelines.
      Establish interface, layout, stage and fixed-state relationships for the exact selected generation.
      Keep metadata canonical and backend realization private; do not copy it into a new descriptor registry.
  [ ] Use existing change publication/dependency links for derived consumer data. Add private subscriptions
      only for actual cached results and remove them with their owner. Propagation must stay local.
  [ ] Refresh owner-following selections on explicit replacement while preserving original borrower/view
      identity and preventing name-reuse retargeting. Cover source/view changes, configuration changes with
      equal descriptor values, detach, local resources, failed mutation and context retirement.
  [ ] Publish coherent facts before subsequent execution; failed refresh withdraws the prepared operation.
      Ordinary content writes must not invalidate structural compatibility. No stale-result fallback,
      automatic retry, registry scan, descriptor query or poll-every-draw recovery path is permitted.
  [ ] Migrate affected producer APIs and all direct consumers together. If a consumer contract cannot change
      independently, include that consumer's phase 4/5 work in this unit before removing the old API.
  [ ] Verify initialization/import failure cleanup, exact-generation compatibility, local change fanout,
      original identity and failed refresh with focused tests; preserve current query/forwarding policy.
  [ ] Update relevant resource, shader and RHI contract documentation to match the completed implementation.
  [ ] Completion gate: producer invariants and actual consumer refresh paths are implemented, with no second
      authority or placeholder notification layer awaiting later work.

Phase 4: Replace binding execution and uniform publication as one unit
  Outcome: shader bindings execute prepared relationships without repeated lifetime/compatibility proof.
  [ ] Prepare backend-neutral active selections, required/optional rules, arrays, views and compatibility
      against the exact shader interface. Keep preparation with the typed operation/renderer owner and
      native realization in the backend. Inactive inputs do no binding work; optional absence clears slots.
  [ ] Separate stable resource structure from captured uniform versions. Reuse owner-local preparation;
      do not globally intern selections or compare every resource merely to discover unchanged capture.
  [ ] Replace BeginBindingExecution and redundant paths in ResourceBindingValidation, ShaderPreparedSubmission
      and ShaderInputValidation. Remove superseded compatibility histories and per-resource temporary
      retain/guard walks, including hidden repeated manager/metadata reads through reference accessors.
  [ ] Migrate immediate and recorded draw/dispatch callers, generated binding owners, internal policy resources
      and local/tool paths together. Preserve recorded captured versions and exact executable identity.
  [ ] Rework BindingReuse and OpenGlStateCache.BindingPublication to process affected slots/tables/domains.
      Distinguish external binding changes from storage/lifetime changes; account for sampler/image selectors,
      program changes, generic-buffer side effects, restoration and deletion/name reuse. Known-equal or
      unrelated observations must not invalidate all publication. Unknown outcomes affect only their domain.
  [ ] Keep uniform allocation/upload/commit with its owner. Refresh for version, epoch and allocation changes;
      preserve single-draw consumption, frame/multi-frame reuse, failed-candidate aborts and GPU-safe last use.
      Different captured versions and multiple executable consumers must not incorrectly share commitment.
  [ ] Preserve exactly-once native forwarding, contained observer errors and partial-publication failure
      semantics. Never retry partially issued native work or certify interrupted publication as complete.
  [ ] Verify unchanged draw/dispatch removes metadata resolution, compatibility reconstruction and resource
      guard work; unrelated registrations do not add work. Verify affected refresh, optional clears, inactive
      inputs, external state restoration, uniform ordering/consumption and preparation/native failure cleanup.
      Compact legitimate binding loops remain allowed; tests must not mandate zero iteration.
  [ ] Reconcile Rendering.ShaderBindingState.md and applicable RHI contracts; delete superseded binding APIs
      and callers in this unit. Check ordinary Release does not depend on diagnostic lifetime checks.
  [ ] Completion gate: every binding route uses the final preparation/publication path, valid Debug/Release
      behavior agrees, and unchanged execution performs no repeated resource-validity proof.

Phase 5: Migrate geometry, pipelines and complete render passes
  Outcome: graphics submission reuses stable geometry/pass relationships under owner lifetimes.
  [ ] Prepare streams, attributes, index layouts, buffer dependencies and reusable fetch limits when their
      declarations/storage/configuration change. Use these facts for necessary dynamic draw arguments;
      indirect contents remain producer-owned, without CPU readback or universal bounds scans.
  [ ] Prepare attachment dimensions/samples/routing, target realization and pipeline/pass compatibility.
      Refresh relational results on attachment/backing, configuration and pipeline changes, including
      changes not represented by descriptor equality alone. Failed preparation cannot remain executable.
  [ ] Refresh framebuffer feedback relationships when selections, attachments or write state change.
      Keep genuinely dynamic restrictions and explicit synchronization; insert no automatic barriers.
  [ ] Replace repeated geometry/pass resource validation and defensive lifetime guards with the phase 2
      owner contracts. Preserve attachment lifetime for the full pass and retain genuine pass sequencing.
      Execute load/store/clear effects on each requested pass, irrespective of cached compatibility.
  [ ] Migrate all immediate/recorded graphics callers, cached draws, engine integrations and local/tool paths
      with obsolete entry-point removal. Integrate preparation with phase 4 without a flattened per-draw
      resource union, a group mechanism or backend-specific types leaking into RHI APIs.
  [ ] Verify dynamic ranges, relevant replacement, attachment relationships, failed refresh, pass effects,
      engine restoration and recorded/cached lifetimes. Check unchanged execution avoids repeated structural
      validation while still producing the required draw/pass effects.
  [ ] Reconcile Rendering.GpuCommands.Contracts.md and affected permanent rendering documentation.
  [ ] Completion gate: the complete graphics path uses prepared structural relationships, explicit owners
      and real dynamic execution work, with no remaining private per-draw lifetime validator in this family.

Phase 6: Migrate transfers, stateful operations and retire obsolete protection
  Outcome: every remaining operation family follows the same contracts without unnecessary plan objects.
  [ ] Apply bounded call-boundary checks to one-off uploads, copies, clears and mip operations. Reuse stable
      format/eligibility relationships where callers already retain preparation; preserve dynamic extents,
      offsets, payload lengths and managed memory safety. Do not allocate a plan/node for every region.
  [ ] Migrate mapping, queries, synchronization and readback. Preserve actual mapping intervals, issued
      generations, readiness, waits/polls, completion and operation effects. Stable metadata never implies
      completed work, unchanged contents, unmapped state or dispensable synchronization.
  [ ] Remove redundant consumer validation/temporary guards in these paths with their callers and tests.
      Keep mapping, allocator and completion protocols that do actual operation/lifetime work; record why
      remaining protections are necessary rather than treating them as exceptions to an admission system.
  [ ] Complete storage/configuration, import, retirement and teardown routes left by the inventory, including
      external/native, recorded and local/tool paths. No family may retain a private polling validator or
      require a future lifecycle fix to make this unit correct.
  [ ] Verify one-off and reusable operations, malformed dynamic payloads, failure cleanup, mapping/query
      state, explicit synchronization and context/GPU-safe retirement using focused behavioral checks.
  [ ] Audit all inventory rows and remove orphaned guard APIs, obsolete compatibility histories, duplicate
      registry resolution and broad publication invalidation. Confirm no shim or native retry remains.
  [ ] Completion gate: all proposal families have completed routes and retained checks have an identified
      structural, dynamic, operational, diagnostic or memory-safety purpose.

Phase 7: Qualify the integrated design and reconcile documentation
  Outcome: the completed system is reviewable with truthful evidence and no temporary support documents.
  [ ] Perform a second source/contract review against every proposal section and the phase 1 migration map.
      Confirm non-owning references, accountable owners, local refresh, singular metadata/cache authorities,
      preserved backend boundaries and absence of universal admission/group machinery.
  [ ] Reconcile Rendering.GpuResources.md, Rendering.ShaderBindingState.md, Rendering.GpuCommands.Contracts.md
      and Rendering.GpuCommands.Proposal.md with the implemented contract. Update affected references and
      Rendering.GpuCommands.todo to record this work's actual status without completing unrelated tasks.
  [ ] Run proportionate integrated automated checks through subagents after coherent implementation changes.
      Distinguish source audit, CPU unit tests, GPU integration tests and ordinary Release/DEBUG diagnostics.
      Reuse valid focused receipts; rerun only where subsequent changes invalidate them or integration requires it.
  [ ] Verify the canonical Release build/package through a subagent, including required dependencies and
      diagnostic exclusion. Record exact commands, results and remaining warnings/failures. No timing benchmark
      or full-suite repetition solely for performance claims is required.
  [ ] Record user-run runtime results separately for affected world rendering, resize, shader reload and
      world exit/reentry/shutdown. Do not launch the game or equate headless/package checks with appearance,
      frame-time or runtime acceptance. If evidence is unavailable, leave that acceptance explicitly pending.
  [ ] Inventory temporary implementation-support documents, explicitly including
      docs/Rendering.BindingAdmission.Migrations.md and any working notes, interim audits or handoff drafts
      created during this plan. Preserve still-required decisions in permanent architecture documentation
      and required receipts in the project's acceptance-evidence destination before deletion.
  [ ] Remove those temporary documents and update all references. Retain this implementation plan,
      Rendering.BindingAdmission.Proposal.md, permanent documentation and required acceptance evidence.
      Do not delete unrelated project documents or classify authoritative documents as temporary.
  [ ] Completion gate: every source requirement is satisfied or an explicit user-approved deferral is recorded;
      automated/package evidence and documentation are reconciled, temporary files are removed, and runtime
      acceptance is accurately marked. No unperformed acceptance or unrelated parent work is marked complete.
